# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Eight Holds Until Number Six Moves

**Story & Context:** The debut runs at 166 BPM. Samira establishes an eight-strike kulintang cycle across two bars and repeats it unchanged for sixteen passes while Adrian gradually increases the complexity of the jungle break underneath. Nadia marks only the first strike of every fourth VALID cycle with one prepared-Rhodes fifth. Rashid plays a single dabakan center hit at the start of each eight-cycle phrase. On the seventeenth repetition, Samira shifts the sixth gong strike forward by one eighth note while leaving all other events untouched. Adrian freezes the break instead of adding a fill, making the small displacement suddenly obvious. The next two cycles enter PROPAGATION as the seventh strike changes pitch in response. Eleanor enters during RECOVERY with one low contrabass-clarinet fundamental when the original eight-strike contour finally returns.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Overhead view of eight bronze gongs arranged beside a faded basketball free-throw line, with a hand-drawn eight-position grid where position six is visibly shifted forward. Rain-dark concrete and a manual scoreboard in shallow focus. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “eight holds,” “six moved,” “keep the count,” and “return the row.”

## 2. Court Line Blue Survives the Break Edit

**Story & Context:** At 172 BPM, Samira keeps the same VALID kulintang pattern for nearly the entire track while Adrian performs the EP’s most aggressive Break Mirror sequence: chopped snares reverse position, kicks disappear, ghost notes multiply, and one-bar edits repeatedly imply false resets. The gong cycle never changes. Nadia plays short suspended-second Rhodes markers only every eight bars so the audience can verify that the cycle boundary has remained stable. Rashid replaces selected kick events with dabakan strokes but never obscures the kulintang. Eleanor contributes clipped low reed notes during the densest break edits. In the final minute, Adrian strips the jungle pattern back to an almost untouched acoustic break while Samira introduces a single missing gong strike for the first time. The attention instantly shifts from the drums to the bronze cycle.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Faded blue basketball-court line continuing perfectly straight beneath a dense collage of chopped drum-waveform fragments, with one bronze gong reflected in a rain puddle. Spoken fragments limited to “blue line stays,” “break moves,” “same cycle,” and “one strike gone.”

## 3. Recovery Begins After the Third Wrong Round

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 160 BPM and begins with no reese bass. Samira presents a slower-feeling two-bar gong cycle while Adrian uses a sparse break with large holes between snares. After eight VALID repetitions, one high gong changes pitch and starts the first DRIFT. The next cycle changes a second event, and the third wrong round shifts the cycle opening by one beat, creating the EP’s widest PROPAGATION. Nadia stays completely silent through all three wrong rounds. Rashid reduces to one muted dabakan stroke every two bars. Eleanor sustains a low contrabass-clarinet tone underneath the final PROPAGATION cycle. RECOVERY begins without a drum fill: Samira simply restores the original contour, Nadia returns with one dry Rhodes octave, and Adrian introduces the first reese note of the track only after the VALID cycle has completed once in full. The recording ends on the next unchanged repetition.

